body:
App.OnStart with twenty Set() calls is slow, order-dependent and hard to maintain. Named formulas (App.Formulas) are evaluated lazily and always up to date, and user-defined functions give you one place for logic you would otherwise copy into ten controls.

## Formulas (App > Formulas property)

```powerfx
// ---- design tokens ----
cBrand = RGBA(0, 90, 158, 1);
cDanger = ColorValue("#B00020");
cMuted = RGBA(96, 94, 92, 1);

// ---- current user, resolved once and cached ----
curUser = LookUp(Employees, Email = Lower(User().Email));
isManager = !IsBlank(curUser) && curUser.Role.Value = "Manager";

// ---- thresholds in one place ----
maxAttachmentMB = 10;
dueSoonDays = 3;

// ---- user-defined functions ----
FormatMoney(amount: Number): Text = Text(amount, "[$-en-US]$#,##0.00");

DaysOverdue(due: Date): Number = Max(0, DateDiff(due, Today(), TimeUnit.Days));

IsDueSoon(due: Date): Boolean =
    due >= Today() && DateDiff(Today(), due, TimeUnit.Days) <= dueSoonDays;

IsValidEmail(s: Text): Boolean = IsMatch(Trim(s), Match.Email);

StatusColor(s: Text): Color =
    Switch(s, "Open", cBrand, "Overdue", cDanger, cMuted);

InitialsOf(fullName: Text): Text =
    Upper(Left(fullName, 1) & Mid(fullName, Find(" ", fullName & " ") + 1, 1));
```

## Using them in controls

```powerfx
// lblAmount.Text
FormatMoney(ThisItem.Amount)

// lblStatus.Color
StatusColor(ThisItem.Status.Value)

// icoWarning.Visible
ThisItem.Status.Value <> "Done" && DaysOverdue(ThisItem.DueDate) > 0

// btnApprove.DisplayMode
If(isManager, DisplayMode.Edit, DisplayMode.Disabled)

// txtEmail.BorderColor
If(IsValidEmail(txtEmail.Text) || IsBlank(txtEmail.Text), cMuted, cDanger)
```

## How to use

1. Open App, choose the Formulas property and paste the block. Every definition ends with a semicolon.
2. Replace the Employees table and Role column with your own.
3. Delete the matching Set(...) lines from App.OnStart. What stays in OnStart is only what truly must run once and has side effects, such as LoadData.
4. User-defined functions have been generally available in canvas apps since September 2025 (the preview switch was removed in Studio version 2508.3). If the formula bar still rejects the function syntax, update the app to the current Studio version and check that the new analysis engine setting under Settings > Updates is on (it is on by default for new apps). In either case, confirm the formulas in the formula bar before you rely on them.

## Common mistakes

- Treating a named formula like a variable. You cannot assign to it with Set, and it re-evaluates when its dependencies change. That is the benefit, but do not use it for values that must stay frozen, such as the time a button was clicked.
- Forgetting parameter types. Every user-defined function parameter needs a type (name: Type) and the function needs a return type.
- Putting behavior (Patch, Navigate, Notify) in a plain function. Functions defined with = are pure. Behavior functions need curly braces and the return type Void and are only valid where behavior formulas are allowed.
- Circular references, for example cBrand defined using a function that uses cBrand. The editor flags it, fix by splitting the definition.
- Heavy data calls inside a function used in a gallery template. They are evaluated for every row.

## Delegation/Compatibility notes

- curUser = LookUp(Employees, Email = Lower(User().Email)) is delegable on Dataverse and SharePoint when Email is a text column; Lower on the right side is a constant and is fine.
- Named formulas are evaluated on demand, which makes the first screen render faster than a long OnStart.
- Function and type names (Number, Text, Boolean, Date, DateTime, Color) follow Power Fx; Boolean is the type name for true/false. The examples use only simple types; passing records and tables through user-defined types was rolled out later than functions, so check the current status before relying on it.
- Named formulas cannot be changed at runtime; for values the user edits keep using Set or UpdateContext.

Validation that only runs on submit is annoying; validation that shouts on first load is worse. This pattern shows messages after the user touched a field and keeps the submit button disabled until everything passes.

## Field rules (define once, reuse everywhere)

Paste each rule inline into the properties that use it, or centralize them as named formulas if your app accepts control references in App.Formulas (confirm in the formula bar). The rule names below are used by the later snippets.

```powerfx
chkTitle = Len(Trim(txtTitle.Text)) >= 3;
chkEmail = IsMatch(Trim(txtEmail.Text), Match.Email);
chkPhone = IsBlank(txtPhone.Text) || IsMatch(txtPhone.Text, "^\+?[0-9 ()\-]{7,20}$");
chkAmount = IfError(Value(txtAmount.Text), 0) > 0 && IfError(Value(txtAmount.Text), 0) <= 10000;
chkDue = !IsBlank(dpDue.SelectedDate) && dpDue.SelectedDate >= Today();
allValid = chkTitle && chkEmail && chkPhone && chkAmount && chkDue;
```

## Error labels (Label.Text and Label.Visible)

```powerfx
// lblTitleError.Visible
varTouchedTitle && !chkTitle

// lblTitleError.Text
"Enter at least 3 characters."

// lblAmountError.Text
If(
    !IsNumeric(txtAmount.Text),
    "Enter a number.",
    "Amount must be between 0.01 and 10,000."
)
```

Mark a field as touched once the user has edited it:

```powerfx
// txtTitle.OnChange   (and OnSelect of the submit button for all fields)
Set(varTouchedTitle, true)

// Submit button: show every message once on a failed attempt
// btnSubmit.OnSelect
If(
    !allValid,
    Set(varTouchedTitle, true);
    Set(varTouchedEmail, true);
    Set(varTouchedAmount, true);
    Notify("Please fix the highlighted fields.", NotificationType.Warning),
    SubmitForm(frmRequest)
)
```

Border color feedback:

```powerfx
// txtEmail.BorderColor
If(varTouchedEmail && !chkEmail, ColorValue("#B00020"), RGBA(138, 136, 134, 1))
```

## With an Edit form control

```powerfx
// frmRequest.OnSuccess
Notify("Request saved", NotificationType.Success);
ResetForm(frmRequest);
Navigate(scrList)

// frmRequest.OnFailure
Notify("Save failed: " & frmRequest.Error, NotificationType.Error)

// DataCard "Title".Required  -> true
// btnSubmit.DisplayMode
If(frmRequest.Valid, DisplayMode.Edit, DisplayMode.Disabled)
```

## How to use

1. Rename controls to match, or edit the names in the chk formulas.
2. Add one error label per field, placed under the input.
3. Reset the touched variables in Screen.OnVisible: Set(varTouchedTitle, false) and so on.
4. Use frmRequest.Valid when you rely on the Form control and its required and length settings; use the chk formulas when you build the layout yourself.

## Common mistakes

- Calling Value() on text that is not a number. Value("abc") yields an error that propagates through And(); wrap it in IfError or test IsNumeric first.
- Regex without anchors. IsMatch(text, "\d{5}") is true if five digits appear anywhere. Use ^...$ or MatchOptions.Complete.
- Using an email regex of your own. Match.Email is built in and maintained.
- Validating only in the app. Add required columns, ranges and length limits in Dataverse or SharePoint as well, because the app is not the only way in.
- Comparing dates with text. dpDue.SelectedDate is a Date value; Today() is also a Date.

## Delegation/Compatibility notes

- Validation formulas run on the client only and involve no delegation.
- IsMatch uses .NET-style regular expressions; the pattern must be a constant (a literal or a named formula that does not depend on a control), otherwise the editor reports an error.
- Match.Email, Match.Digit and MatchOptions.Complete are predefined enumerations.

Field workers lose coverage. Canvas apps can keep working if you cache reads and queue writes in local collections stored with SaveData.

## Startup (App.OnStart)

```powerfx
If(
    Connection.Connected,
    ClearCollect(colTasks, Filter(Tasks, AssignedTo.Email = User().Email));
    SaveData(colTasks, "tasksCache"),
    LoadData(colTasks, "tasksCache", true)
);
LoadData(colPending, "pendingEdits", true)
```

The third argument true means "do not raise an error if the file does not exist yet" which is what you want on the first launch.

## Save an edit locally first (btnSave.OnSelect)

```powerfx
Collect(
    colPending,
    {
        LocalId: GUID(),
        TaskId: varTask.ID,
        NewStatus: ddStatus.Selected.Value,
        Note: txtNote.Text,
        EditedAt: Now()
    }
);
SaveData(colPending, "pendingEdits");
// reflect the change in the cached list so the UI updates immediately
Patch(colTasks, LookUp(colTasks, ID = varTask.ID), {Status: {Value: ddStatus.Selected.Value}});
SaveData(colTasks, "tasksCache");
Notify(If(Connection.Connected, "Saved, syncing...", "Saved offline, will sync later"), NotificationType.Information)
```

## Sync the queue (Timer.OnTimerEnd, Duration 30000, Repeat true, AutoStart true)

```powerfx
If(
    Connection.Connected && !IsEmpty(colPending) && !varSyncing,
    Set(varSyncing, true);
    // iterate over a snapshot, because rows are removed from colPending inside the loop
    ClearCollect(colBatch, colPending);
    ForAll(
        colBatch As P,
        // true/false keep both branches the same type, because the IfError result ends up in the ForAll output table
        IfError(
            Patch(Tasks, LookUp(Tasks, ID = P.TaskId), {Status: {Value: P.NewStatus}, Notes: P.Note});
            Remove(colPending, LookUp(colPending, LocalId = P.LocalId));
            true,
            // keep the row in the queue, try again next tick
            Collect(colSyncErrors, {LocalId: P.LocalId, Message: FirstError.Message});
            false
        )
    );
    SaveData(colPending, "pendingEdits");
    Set(varSyncing, false)
)
```

Status indicator for the header:

```powerfx
// lblSync.Text
If(
    !Connection.Connected, "Offline - " & CountRows(colPending) & " pending",
    CountRows(colPending) > 0, "Syncing " & CountRows(colPending) & "...",
    "All changes saved"
)
```

## How to use

1. Publish and test in the Power Apps mobile app: switch to airplane mode, edit, reconnect and watch the queue drain.
2. Show colSyncErrors on an admin or diagnostics screen so failed rows can be inspected.
3. Add a "Sync now" button with the same logic as the timer, for impatient users.

## Common mistakes

- Testing only in Power Apps Studio. SaveData and LoadData behave differently there and in the browser player; the device storage is the reliable target.
- Storing sensitive data. The local file is not a secure vault. Cache only what the user may keep on the device.
- Modifying a collection while iterating it in ForAll. The sync formula loops over the snapshot colBatch and removes rows from colPending by the stable LocalId key.
- No conflict strategy. Two people editing the same row offline produce last-writer-wins. Compare Modified with EditedAt before patching if that matters.
- Letting the cache grow without bound. Filter at load time and clear old rows.

## Delegation/Compatibility notes

- Filter with AssignedTo.Email: confirm there is no delegation warning for your connector; the data cached by ClearCollect is capped by the data row limit (500 by default).
- Connection.Connected, SaveData, LoadData and GUID are available in canvas apps; SaveData storage is per app and per device.
- For heavy offline needs with relational data, use the Dataverse mobile offline profile for model-driven or canvas apps instead of hand-rolled queues.
